          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "A note says hypertension and chronic kidney disease. Does the classification assume a relationship between them, and what changes if it does?",
          "answer": "**Short answer:** Yes. Under the \"with\" convention, a note listing hypertension and chronic kidney disease (CKD) is classified as a linked condition unless the record says the two are unrelated. The classification does not need the note to state the link.\n\n**The convention.** The diabetes coding summary describes this as a general rule rather than a diabetes-only one. Where the alphabetic index links two conditions with \"with,\" the classification reads that as \"associated with\" or \"due to\" and presumes the relationship. The document's example is type 2 diabetes plus CKD, coded as diabetic kidney disease unless the documentation says the two are unrelated (coding-type-2-diabetes-e11). I found this wording only in that document, so treat it as the corpus's description of the rule, not a complete statement of it.\n\n**What changes when the link is assumed.** The hypertension coding summary says that when hypertension occurs with CKD, the code moves from I10 (hypertension alone) to I12, and a second code gives the stage of kidney disease. If both heart and kidney involvement are present, the code is I13 (coding-hypertension-i10). The chapter overview says these combination codes fold hypertension together with heart or kidney disease attributed to it (coding-icd10-chapter-09-circulatory). The stage code comes from the N18 range in the genitourinary chapter (coding-icd10-chapter-14-genitourinary).\n\n**What changes when the link is not assumed.** Hypertension with no linked organ disease stays I10. Hypertension attributed to a specific underlying cause, such as a kidney or hormonal cause, is coded as secondary hypertension in I15, together with the cause (coding-hypertension-i10). The corpus does not say how a note must word a cause to trigger I15 instead of I12, so that decision belongs to the coder and clinician.\n\n**What I could not cover.** My search for the exact documentation wording needed to override the presumption did not complete, so I cannot tell you the precise phrasing a record needs. Coders and clinicians make these assignments using the full code set and their facility's rules. If you have a specific note, a medical coder or the clinician who wrote it can confirm how it was classified.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
